# r, THE REMAINDER VECTOR, WITH ITS SIGN DERIVED AND ITS EXPOSURE MEASURED. One row per layer
# that states both a demand and a nameplate. The key is (filing, layer) and every downstream fit
# question reads this relation.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ IT CHOOSES BETWEEN TWO ARITHMETICS AND THE CHOICE IS THE WHOLE FILE. On a layer built
#    from parts, `r` is `F Φ r_parts - e_n + e_d`, each part's own remainder converted once, so
#    a shared conversion factor appears once per term. Everywhere else `r` is `n - d` from the
#    two filed totals with the bounds crossed. The two agree on every layer whose factors are
#    point values, which is all but the composed layers carrying a factor with spread, and there
#    the crossed difference counts that factor's spread twice and comes out wider.
#
# ⛔ SO THE CROSSED DIFFERENCE IS A BOUND ON THOSE LAYERS AND NOT THEIR REMAINDER, and taking it
#   as the remainder makes `derived_fit` read `transition` on a filing that is correctly
#   `clearance`. checks/fit_disagrees compares the filed sign against `derived_fit`, so the
#   flagship rule is what would have reported a document that is right.
#
# ⭐⭐ `derived_fit` AND `exposure` ARE RECOMPUTED FROM WHICHEVER `r` IS IN FORCE, never carried
#    across from the other. `exposure` is `greatest(-r_low, 0)`, which is `d_high - n_low` on the
#    differenced reading and generalises to the pivoted one without a second expression.
#
# ⭐⭐ `derived_fit` IS A COMPARISON OF TWO RANGES AND NOT OF TWO POINTS -- ISO 286's own
#    criterion, borrowed intact. `clearance` = supply exceeds demand ACROSS THE WHOLE RANGE.
#    `interference` = demand exceeds supply across the whole range. `transition` = the ranges
#    overlap, so which one wins depends on where in the range you land. That third case is the
#    one a two-valued encoding cannot name, and the corpus contains it.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE ARM ORDER IN `derived_fit` IS A DECISION AND NOT AN ARTEFACT. The three criteria are
#    disjoint everywhere except at one pair, a POINT nameplate equal to a POINT demand, where
#    all three admit it: chaining the first two forces `n_low >= d_high >= d_low >= n_high >=
#    n_low`, so every bound is equal and `r = [0, 0, 0]`. The first arm wins and returns
#    `clearance`, which is ISO 286's answer for the line-to-line case and the only one of the
#    three whose downstream reading is right: nothing went unmet, so checks/clearance_with_unserved
#    rules out the two holders that cannot be there. ⚠️ Swapping these two arms would silently
#    reclassify that pair, and `Fit`'s annotation says so in prose rather than leaving it here.
#
# ⭐ `exposure` IS WHAT THE FILING'S OWN NUMBERS SAY COULD HAVE GONE UNSERVED: the largest
#   demand against the smallest supply, floored at zero. It is not a measurement anybody made;
#   it is the ceiling their two measurements imply, which is why the rule built on it compares
#   against what they ADMIT went unserved rather than against a reading.
-- layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc, overridden by composition/fused_remainders.sqlc where a
-- composed layer owes an exact remainder.
SELECT b.filing, b.layer,
       b.sign, b.sign_absent,
       b.absorber_taxonomy, b.absorber_value,
       b.d_low, b.d_mode, b.d_high, b.unit,
       b.n_low, b.n_mode, b.n_high, b.amount_unit,
       coalesce(f.pivoted_low,  b.r_low)  AS r_low,
       coalesce(f.pivoted_mode, b.r_mode) AS r_mode,
       coalesce(f.pivoted_high, b.r_high) AS r_high,
       CASE WHEN coalesce(f.pivoted_low,  b.r_low)  >= 0 THEN 'clearance'
            WHEN coalesce(f.pivoted_high, b.r_high) <= 0 THEN 'interference'
            ELSE 'transition' END AS derived_fit,
       greatest(-coalesce(f.pivoted_low, b.r_low), 0) AS exposure,
       b.lumpy, b.quantum_mode, b.quantum_unit,
       (f.pivoted_low IS NOT NULL) AS pivoted
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
) b
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/fused_remainders.sqlc)
) f ON f.composition = b.filing AND f.composed_layer = b.layer
